// From-zero primers for the second group of models (Jervis 1978, Kydd 2000, Slantchev 2003, Rubinstein 1982).
// Written for a reader who has never seen game theory. Every claim about what an author argues carries a page
// in the published article, and every page was opened for this file (see FACTCHECK-primers-b.md). Numbers quoted
// in the steps are what the page's own model code computes for that step's settings.

export const PRIMERS_B = {
  jervis78: {
    question: 'Why do states that want only to keep what they have still end up in arms races and conflict with each other, and what makes it easier for them to cooperate anyway (pp. 167, 170)?',
    puzzle: 'If every state were content with the status quo, you might expect peace, since nobody wants to attack. But there is no world government to enforce promises, so a state that cooperates can be ruined if the other side does not (p. 167). Worse, “many of the means by which a state tries to increase its security decrease the security of others” (p. 169). So states that fear only for their own safety can still frighten each other into arming and fighting. Jervis asks the question directly: “why are we not all dead?” (p. 170).',
    others: [
      { name: 'Arms buildups reveal aggressive intentions', claim: 'If another state objects to my defensive arms, or arms heavily itself, it must be planning aggression.', take: 'Jervis says a state that does not understand the security dilemma reasons this way, as in the Acheson–Connally exchange on NATO (pp. 181-182). A state arming heavily “might be not an aggressor but merely an insecure state,” and treating it as an aggressor can “deepen the dilemma and create needless conflict” (p. 212).', src: 'pp. 181-182, 212' },
      { name: 'Germany in 1914: aggression or defense?', claim: 'Historians ask whether Germany unleashed the war deliberately to become a world power or backed Austria to defend a weakening ally.', take: 'Jervis calls the question “to some extent … misleading”: because leaders believed the offense had the advantage, war looked like the best route both to expansion and to avoiding a drastic loss of influence (p. 191).', src: 'p. 191' },
      { name: 'More arms always mean more security', claim: 'When in doubt, a state can “play it safe” by building more weapons; the only cost is the money.', take: 'Statesmen who hold this belief set their security requirements too high, because arming can lower security by alarming others. Two status-quo states that think this way are likely to end up in more conflict than the situation requires (p. 182).', src: 'p. 182' },
      { name: 'Economic ties keep the peace', claim: 'Trade makes war too costly to fight (Norman Angell’s argument before 1914).', take: 'For states with large and diverse economies, “the gains from economic exchange are rarely if ever sufficient to prevent war.” Angell was wrong about 1914, and the United States was Japan’s most important trading partner before 1941 (p. 177).', src: 'p. 177' },
      { name: 'Offense and defense cannot be told apart', claim: 'Salvador de Madariaga: “A weapon is either offensive or defensive according to which end of it you are looking at.”', take: 'Jervis grants that “no simple and unambiguous definition is possible” (p. 201), but holds that “some distinctions are possible”: fortifications, for example, cannot occupy enemy territory (p. 203).', src: 'pp. 201-203' },
      { name: 'It always pays to strike first', claim: 'Herman Kahn warned that ultimatums were too dangerous because they would likely touch off a pre-emptive strike.', take: 'Kahn “incorrectly assumes that it is always advantageous to strike first.” When the defense has the advantage, a state that fears attack prepares to receive it instead of striking (p. 190).', src: 'p. 190' },
    ],
    terms: [
      { term: 'Game', plain: 'A situation where what happens to you depends on your choice and on someone else’s. Here, two states each decide whether to arm.' },
      { term: 'Player', plain: 'A decision maker in the game. Here each player is a state. The page calls one of them “you” and the other “the other.”' },
      { term: 'Cooperate (C) and defect (D)', plain: 'The two choices. Cooperate means staying lightly armed or keeping an agreement. Defect means arming or cheating. Jervis’s international analogue of cooperation is “cooperation and disarmament” (p. 167).' },
      { term: 'Payoff', plain: 'How good an outcome is for a player. Bigger is better. The table shows each cell as (your payoff, the other’s payoff).' },
      { term: 'CC, DC, DD, CD', plain: 'The four outcomes, written as your move then the other’s. CC: both cooperate. DC: you defect while the other cooperates, so you exploit it. DD: both defect, which means an arms race and a high risk of war. CD: you cooperate while the other defects, so you are exploited (p. 167). The four sliders set how much each outcome is worth to you.' },
      { term: 'Preference ordering', plain: 'The ranking of outcomes from best to worst. Jervis gives only rankings, not amounts (p. 171). The page uses notional numbers such as 4, 3, 2, 1 as stand-ins, so only their order matters.' },
      { term: 'Stag Hunt', plain: 'Rousseau’s story: hunters who cooperate trap a stag and all eat well, but anyone who chases a rabbit instead leaves the others with nothing. The ranking is CC > DC > DD > CD. Both sides most want mutual cooperation, yet each will defect if it expects the other to (pp. 167-168).' },
      { term: 'Prisoner’s Dilemma', plain: 'Here the ranking is DC > CC > DD > CD. Each side most wants to exploit the other, so no outcome is everyone’s first choice. Played once, “the only rational response is to defect” (p. 171).' },
      { term: 'Chicken', plain: 'The game you get when war is so costly that DD becomes each side’s worst outcome. Each side tries to exploit the other, but both share an interest in avoiding a total breakdown (pp. 177-178).' },
      { term: 'Deadlock, Harmony, Other ordering', plain: 'The page’s labels for other rankings you can reach with the sliders. Deadlock: both prefer mutual defection to mutual cooperation. Harmony: cooperating is best whatever the other does. These labels come from the page, not from Jervis.' },
      { term: 'Dominant strategy', plain: 'A choice that is best no matter what the other player does. In a one-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma, defecting is dominant.' },
      { term: 'Equilibrium (pure)', plain: 'A pair of choices where neither player could do better by changing its own choice alone. The table outlines these cells. A Stag Hunt has two: both cooperate and both defect.' },
      { term: 'Mixed equilibrium', plain: 'An equilibrium in which each side randomizes, cooperating with some probability. The page reports it when one exists.' },
      { term: 'q (your estimate that the other cooperates)', plain: 'A probability between 0 and 1: how likely you think it is that the other state cooperates. This is Jervis’s third lever, each side’s expectation of the other (p. 171).' },
      { term: 'Expected value of C and of D', plain: 'The average payoff you expect from a choice, given q. Cooperating is worth q × CC + (1 − q) × CD. Defecting is worth q × DC + (1 − q) × DD. A state compares the two (p. 179). The two lines in the chart plot them.' },
      { term: 'Trust needed, q*', plain: 'The value of q at which cooperating and defecting are worth the same, where the two lines cross. Above q*, cooperating pays more. The page computes it from the four payoffs.' },
      { term: 'Wait and see (moving second)', plain: 'If being exploited is not ruinous, a state can wait to see what the other does before choosing. Jervis says a low cost of CD turns a simultaneous game into one where an actor “can make his choice after the other has moved” (p. 172). The toggle lets you move second.' },
      { term: 'Repeated play and δ', plain: 'The game is played again and again (p. 171). δ (delta), between 0 and 1, is how much a state values the next round compared with this one. Near 1 means very patient.' },
      { term: 'Repeat-play threshold δ (grim trigger)', plain: 'A textbook rule, not Jervis’s own formula: each side cooperates until the other cheats once, then defects forever. Cooperation holds if δ ≥ (DC − CC)/(DC − DD).' },
      { term: 'Status-quo state', plain: 'A state that wants to keep what it has and does not seek to expand. Jervis’s question is about these states (p. 167).' },
      { term: 'Anarchy', plain: 'The absence of any authority above states that could make and enforce international law (p. 167).' },
      { term: 'Security dilemma', plain: 'The fact that “many of the means by which a state tries to increase its security decrease the security of others” (p. 169).' },
      { term: 'Offense-defense balance', plain: 'Whether attacking or defending is easier. The offense has the advantage when “it is easier to destroy the other’s army and take its territory than it is to defend one’s own” (p. 187).' },
      { term: 'Offense-defense differentiation (distinguishable postures)', plain: 'Whether weapons and policies that protect a state can be told apart from those that could be used to attack. When they can, a state can make itself safer without threatening others (pp. 187, 199).' },
      { term: 'Four worlds', plain: 'The four combinations of those two variables: offense or defense has the advantage, and postures are or are not distinguishable (p. 211). The “Four worlds” tab shows them as a 2 × 2 grid.' },
      { term: 'Notional', plain: 'Illustrative numbers with no real-world measurement behind them. All four payoff sliders are notional.' },
    ],
    steps: [
      { t: 'Read the table', text: 'Start on the “Payoffs and expectations” tab. The table has your two choices as rows and the other state’s as columns. With these numbers the game is a Stag Hunt: both cooperating (4) is best for each, and cooperating alone (1) is worst. Two cells are outlined because both are equilibria: if each expects the other to cooperate, both do, and if each expects defection, both defect. Nothing in the payoffs picks between them. At q = 0.6 cooperating is worth 2.8 and defecting 2.6, so you cooperate.', set: { v: 'game', cc: 4, dc: 3, dd: 2, cd: 1, q: 0.6, seq: 0, rep: 0 } },
      { t: 'Lower your trust', text: 'Now you think the other cooperates only 30% of the time. Look at the chart: left of q* = 0.5, where the two lines cross, the cooperate line lies below the defect line. Cooperating is worth 1.9 and defecting 2.3, so you defect, even though both sides would prefer mutual cooperation. Jervis: “Unless each person thinks that the others will cooperate, he himself will not” (p. 168).', set: { v: 'game', cc: 4, dc: 3, dd: 2, cd: 1, q: 0.3, seq: 0, rep: 0 } },
      { t: 'Make being exploited cheaper', text: 'Raise CD from 1 to 1.9: a state that is cheated loses little, perhaps because it is large, remote or hard to conquer. q* drops to about 0.09, and at the same 30% trust cooperating now pays (2.53 against 2.3). Jervis says the fear of being exploited “most strongly drives the security dilemma” (p. 172).', set: { v: 'game', cc: 4, dc: 3, dd: 2, cd: 1.9, q: 0.3, seq: 0, rep: 0 } },
      { t: 'Wait and see', text: 'Put CD back at 1, keep trust at 0.3, and switch on “move second.” Now you see the other’s choice before you make yours: you would answer cooperation with cooperation and defection with defection. Knowing that, the first mover cooperates safely. This is Jervis’s point that when being exploited is not ruinous, a state “can wait to see what the other will do” (p. 172).', set: { v: 'game', cc: 4, dc: 3, dd: 2, cd: 1, q: 0.3, seq: 1, rep: 0 } },
      { t: 'Switch to a Prisoner’s Dilemma', text: 'Make exploiting the other the top outcome (DC = 4, CC = 3). Now defecting pays more whatever you expect: even at 90% trust, defecting is worth 3.8 and cooperating 2.8. Only mutual defection is outlined. Moving second does not help either, because you would exploit a cooperator. Played once, “the only rational response is to defect” (p. 171).', set: { v: 'game', cc: 3, dc: 4, dd: 2, cd: 1, q: 0.9, seq: 0, rep: 0 } },
      { t: 'Play it again and again', text: 'Switch on repeated play with δ = 0.6. Jervis notes that if the game “is repeated indefinitely,” defection is no longer the only rational choice (p. 171). The page uses the standard grim-trigger rule: here cooperation holds when δ ≥ 0.5, so it holds at 0.6. Drag δ below 0.5 and it collapses.', set: { v: 'game', cc: 3, dc: 4, dd: 2, cd: 1, q: 0.9, seq: 0, rep: 1, dl: 0.6 } },
      { t: 'The worst world', text: 'Open the “Four worlds” tab. Offense has the advantage and offensive postures cannot be told from defensive ones. Status-quo states buy the same arms as aggressors and, since attacking is the best way to protect what they have, act like aggressors. Arms races are likely and first-strike incentives turn crises into wars. Jervis says Europe before 1914 bore “more than a passing resemblance” to this world (p. 211).', set: { v: 'worlds', od: 'off', dist: 0 } },
      { t: 'The best world', text: 'Defense has the advantage and postures can be told apart. A status-quo state has no reason to buy offensive forces, and an aggressor gives notice by the posture it adopts (p. 214). Click the other two cells to see the mixed cases. Of the world where postures look alike but defense dominates, Jervis writes: “This world is the one that comes closest to matching most periods in history” (p. 213).', set: { v: 'worlds', od: 'def', dist: 1 } },
    ],
    answer: 'Cooperation is more likely the more mutual cooperation pays, the less being exploited costs, the less exploiting the other pays, the more mutual defection costs, and the more each side expects the other to cooperate (p. 171). But the heart of the problem is military. When offensive and defensive postures differ, a state can make itself more secure without making others less secure. When the defense has the advantage, one state’s gain costs others little (p. 187). When the offense has the advantage and postures look alike, status-quo states must behave like aggressors (pp. 187, 211).',
    limits: 'Jervis ranks outcomes but does not measure them (p. 171), so the page’s numbers are stand-ins. He grants that “there is no simple way to determine” whether the offense or the defense is dominant (p. 197). And of Europe before 1914 he notes that “the powers’ ability to avoid war in a series of earlier crises cannot be easily explained” by his theory (p. 211). The repeated-game threshold on the page is a textbook result, not Jervis’s.',
  },

  kydd00: {
    question: 'Can a state that really is trustworthy convince a suspicious rival of that, when an untrustworthy state would want to send the same message, and if so, when and how (pp. 326, 330)?',
    puzzle: 'Many explanations of war start from mistrust: each side fears the other will exploit any cooperation, so both arm, even though both would prefer peace (p. 325). If that fear is mistaken, both sides gain by dispelling it (p. 325). But words are cheap. An aggressive state would also claim to be peaceful: Kydd’s example is Hitler’s claim that he had no more territorial demands in Europe: such claims are unpersuasive “because there is nothing to prevent untrustworthy types from making them” (p. 340). So what can a trustworthy state do that an untrustworthy one would not copy?',
    others: [
      { name: 'Structural realism: trust is scarce', claim: 'There is “little room for trust among states” because intentions are hard to discern, so fear “can never be reduced to a trivial level” (Mearsheimer).', take: 'Kydd’s one-round trust game “seems to validate the traditional structural realist gloom” (p. 333). But once a state can make a costly first gesture, cooperation becomes possible at much lower levels of trust (pp. 339-340). This “challenges adherents of structural realist and security dilemma-based explanations” to say why reassurance fails in a given case (p. 327).', src: 'pp. 325, 327, 333' },
      { name: 'Defensive realism and the security dilemma', claim: 'Recurring security dilemmas force states to arm against each other even though both would prefer mutual cooperation.', take: 'Kydd does not rule out explanations of conflict based on mistrust. He asks those who advance them to explain why reassurance was blocked. For example, if mistrust caused the Cold War, why could the two sides not reassure each other until the late 1980s (p. 327)?', src: 'pp. 325, 327' },
      { name: 'Signaling through defensive weapons', claim: 'Jervis (1978) and later offense-defense theorists: security seekers can reveal themselves by buying defensive rather than offensive forces.', take: 'Kydd says this strand highlights one possible avenue and introduces the idea of costly signals, but “fails to pursue the latter insight in depth” (p. 330).', src: 'pp. 328-330' },
      { name: 'GRIT and the psychological school', claim: 'Osgood and Etzioni: mistrust drives arms races, and small unilateral gestures can reverse it. Osgood’s GRIT is Graduated Reciprocation In Tension-reduction.', take: 'GRIT is “fifteen rules with no clear prioritization or underlying theory,” and one of them, keeping initiatives unpredictable, is “totally unnecessary” from a costly signaling view (p. 330). Still, Kydd’s result that a more fearful state must start with a smaller gesture “lends support to the intuition, formulated by Osgood in the GRIT strategy” (p. 340).', src: 'pp. 328, 330, 340' },
      { name: 'Reassurance rarely works', claim: 'Downs and Rocke: reassurance is risky and will seldom succeed, since the other side may take advantage of concessions.', take: 'Their results come from a numerical simulation and so are “less general than might be desirable” (p. 330 n. 27). Kydd’s game, by contrast, “powerfully reaffirms the rationality of reassurance” (p. 340).', src: 'pp. 329-330, 340' },
      { name: 'The Cold War ended because of Soviet decline', claim: 'Gorbachev’s concessions were simply a product of Soviet weakness.', take: 'Kydd accepts that weakness partly inspired the concessions, but argues that “economic weakness alone did not dictate the depth of rapprochement.” Military spending could have been cut while keeping a tight grip on Eastern Europe (p. 350).', src: 'p. 350' },
    ],
    terms: [
      { term: 'Player', plain: 'A decision maker in the game. Here, two states called player 1 and player 2.' },
      { term: 'Cooperate and defect', plain: 'The two moves in each round. Cooperating risks being exploited; defecting avoids that risk but gives up the gains from cooperation.' },
      { term: 'Payoff', plain: 'How good an outcome is for a player. Mutual defection is set to 0, so every other payoff is measured against it (p. 332).' },
      { term: 'Type: nice or mean', plain: 'Kydd’s players come in two kinds. A nice (trustworthy) type prefers to return cooperation. A mean (untrustworthy) type prefers to exploit it (p. 331). Each player knows its own type but not the other’s.' },
      { term: 'Stag Hunt and Prisoner’s Dilemma preferences', plain: 'Nice types have Stag Hunt preferences: mutual cooperation is their top outcome. Mean types have Prisoner’s Dilemma preferences: exploiting a cooperator beats mutual cooperation (p. 331 n. 30).' },
      { term: 'R (reward)', plain: 'The payoff for mutual cooperation. R<sub>1N</sub> is the nice player 1’s reward, R<sub>1M</sub> the mean player 1’s (p. 331). Sliders R<sub>1N</sub> and R<sub>1M</sub>.' },
      { term: 'T (temptation)', plain: 'The payoff for defecting while the other cooperates, that is, for exploiting it. T<sub>1M</sub> is the mean player 1’s temptation (p. 331). Slider T<sub>1M</sub>.' },
      { term: 'S (sucker’s payoff)', plain: 'What a player loses when it cooperates and the other defects. The payoff is −S, so a bigger S means being exploited hurts more (p. 332). Sliders S<sub>1N</sub> and S<sub>1M</sub>. The page’s default S = 1 is Kydd’s sucker’s payoff of −1 (p. 336).' },
      { term: 'Trust (p<sub>2</sub>)', plain: 'Player 1’s belief, as a probability, that player 2 is the nice type. Kydd defines trust exactly this way: the probability that the other side is trustworthy (pp. 326, 331). The x-axis of the chart and the first slider.' },
      { term: 'Prior belief', plain: 'Trust before any gesture is made. Kydd notes it can come from past experience: France, invaded by Germany three times in seventy years, distrusted it after 1945 (p. 331).' },
      { term: 'Trust game', plain: 'Kydd’s one-round game. Player 1 must decide whether to cooperate without learning anything about player 2 first (pp. 331-333).' },
      { term: 'p* (trust-game threshold)', plain: 'The trust at which cooperating in the one-round game just pays: p* = S/(R + S). Above it player 1 cooperates, below it player 1 defects (p. 332). With the default payoffs p* = 0.33, the first vertical line on the chart.' },
      { term: 'Reassurance game', plain: 'The trust game split into two rounds. Player 1 first chooses how much of the relationship to stake in round 1 and cooperates there. Then player 2 moves first in round 2, the rest of the relationship (pp. 333-335).' },
      { term: 'Gesture size (α)', plain: 'The weight of the first round, between 0 and 1; round 2 is worth 1 − α. A bigger α is a riskier, more costly gesture. Kydd’s example: Gorbachev’s concessions on intermediate-range nuclear forces in 1987 were a first round worth α, with strategic arms still unsettled (pp. 334-335). The y-axis of the chart.' },
      { term: 'Costly signal', plain: 'An action so costly or risky that an untrustworthy state would hesitate to take it, so taking it is evidence of trustworthiness (p. 326).' },
      { term: 'Cheap talk', plain: 'A signal with too little cost to separate the types, because both would send it. It changes no one’s beliefs (pp. 326, 338).' },
      { term: 'Separating equilibrium', plain: 'An outcome where the two types act differently, so the action reveals the type. Here the nice player 1 makes a gesture of size α* and the mean one makes none (p. 336). This is where reassurance happens (p. 337).' },
      { term: 'Pooling equilibrium', plain: 'An outcome where both types act the same, so the action reveals nothing. Kydd finds four kinds in his game; the page draws only the separating one (pp. 336-337).' },
      { term: 'Perfect Bayesian equilibrium', plain: 'Kydd’s solution concept (p. 336). Each player’s moves are its best reply to the other’s, and beliefs are updated by Bayes’ rule, the arithmetic for revising a probability after seeing evidence.' },
      { term: 'Lower bound: mean type won’t fake it', plain: 'The smallest gesture a mean type will not fake. Below it, the mean type would make the gesture too, to lure player 2 into cooperating and then exploit it (pp. 337-338).' },
      { term: 'Upper bound: nice type will risk it', plain: 'The largest gesture a nice type will still risk. Above it, the nice type fears being exploited by a mean player 2 too much (p. 338).' },
      { term: 'Separating gestures (shaded band)', plain: 'Gesture sizes between the two bounds, which reveal a nice player 1.' },
      { term: 'p<sub>2</sub>*<sup>M</sup> (upper bound on trust)', plain: 'The trust-game threshold computed with the mean type’s payoffs, S<sub>1M</sub>/(R<sub>1M</sub> + S<sub>1M</sub>). Above it even a mean player 1 would cooperate in round 1, so a gesture no longer tells the types apart (p. 338). The second vertical line.' },
      { term: 'Lower bound on trust', plain: 'The lowest trust at which a separating gesture exists. With Kydd’s example payoffs it is zero, so reassurance works however fearful the players are (pp. 338-339).' },
      { term: 'p<sub>2</sub>*<sup>N</sup> and the Proposition', plain: 'p<sub>2</sub>*<sup>N</sup> is the threshold for the nice type. Kydd’s Proposition: if the nice type will run a greater risk for cooperation than the mean type (p<sub>2</sub>*<sup>N</sup> < p<sub>2</sub>*<sup>M</sup>), a separating equilibrium exists (p. 339).' },
      { term: 'GRIT', plain: 'Osgood’s Graduated Reciprocation In Tension-reduction: begin with small cooperative gestures and build up as trust grows (pp. 328, 340).' },
    ],
    steps: [
      { t: 'One round, no second chances', text: 'Start with Kydd’s example payoffs and trust p<sub>2</sub> = 0.25. In the one-round trust game player 1 cooperates only if trust exceeds p* = 0.33, the first vertical line on the chart. At 0.25 it does not, so two states that might both be nice never cooperate. Kydd says this game “seems to validate the traditional structural realist gloom” (p. 333).', set: { p2: 0.25, a: 0.3, RN: 2, SN: 1, TM: 2, RM: 1, SM: 1 } },
      { t: 'A gesture that is too cheap', text: 'Now player 1 can open with a gesture of size α. The chart’s y-axis is that size, and the dot is your choice. At α = 0.3 the dot sits below the lower-bound line: the gesture is so cheap that a mean player 1 would make it too, to fool player 2 into cooperating and then exploit it. Signals with too little cost are “cheap talk” (p. 338).', set: { p2: 0.25, a: 0.3, RN: 2, SN: 1, TM: 2, RM: 1, SM: 1 } },
      { t: 'A gesture of the right size', text: 'Raise α to 0.85, inside the shaded band from 0.80 to 0.89. Now only a nice player 1 will make the gesture, so making it reveals the type. A nice player 2 then cooperates, and the two nice types end up cooperating fully (p. 337), at a level of trust where the one-round game failed.', set: { p2: 0.25, a: 0.85, RN: 2, SN: 1, TM: 2, RM: 1, SM: 1 } },
      { t: 'A gesture that is too big', text: 'Raise α to 0.95, above the upper-bound line. Staking this much on a player 2 who may be mean is too risky even for a nice player 1. “In order to work, the signals must be costly, but not too costly” (p. 340).', set: { p2: 0.25, a: 0.95, RN: 2, SN: 1, TM: 2, RM: 1, SM: 1 } },
      { t: 'More fear, smaller first step', text: 'Cut trust to 0.1. Both lines fall, and the band moves down to about 0.71–0.74; α = 0.73 works. The more fearful player 1 is of player 2, the smaller its first gesture must be. Kydd says this “lends support to the intuition, formulated by Osgood in the GRIT strategy,” that deep mistrust calls for small first steps (p. 340).', set: { p2: 0.1, a: 0.73, RN: 2, SN: 1, TM: 2, RM: 1, SM: 1 } },
      { t: 'When trust is already high', text: 'Raise trust to 0.6, past p<sub>2</sub>*<sup>M</sup> = 0.5. Now even a mean player 1 would cooperate in round 1, so no gesture tells the types apart and the band disappears (p. 338). Reassurance is not needed here: trust is above p*, and Kydd’s pooling equilibria cover these cases of a relatively trusting player (p. 337).', set: { p2: 0.6, a: 0.9, RN: 2, SN: 1, TM: 2, RM: 1, SM: 1 } },
      { t: 'A mean type that likes cooperation', text: 'Back at trust 0.25, raise the mean type’s reward for cooperation R<sub>1M</sub> to 1.9. It is now more tempted to mimic the nice type, and the band shrinks to about 0.879–0.889. Kydd stresses that the mean type’s incentives matter as much as the nice type’s, even though in equilibrium the mean type never reassures anyone (pp. 339-340).', set: { p2: 0.25, a: 0.88, RN: 2, SN: 1, TM: 2, RM: 1.9, SM: 1 } },
      { t: 'A nice type that fears being the sucker', text: 'Put R<sub>1M</sub> back at 1 and raise the nice type’s cost of being the sucker S<sub>1N</sub> to 3. The nice type now needs more trust than the mean type to cooperate (p<sub>2</sub>*<sup>N</sup> = 0.6 > p<sub>2</sub>*<sup>M</sup> = 0.5), so Kydd’s condition fails and no gesture can separate them (p. 339).', set: { p2: 0.25, a: 0.85, RN: 2, SN: 3, TM: 2, RM: 1, SM: 1 } },
    ],
    answer: 'Yes, often. A trustworthy state can reassure with a costly signal: a first cooperative gesture big enough that an untrustworthy state would not fake it, yet small enough that the trustworthy state will risk it (pp. 337-338, 340). Such a gesture exists when the trustworthy type is willing to run greater risks for cooperation than the untrustworthy type (p. 339). Then states can cooperate at levels of trust too low for cooperation in a one-shot game, and the more fearful they are, the smaller the first step must be (pp. 339-340). Kydd reads Gorbachev’s concessions, including the 1987 INF treaty, the 1988 withdrawal from Afghanistan and the 1989 noninterference in the Eastern European revolutions, as such signals (pp. 327, 350).',
    limits: 'Kydd notes that reality is “more complicated than the model”: there were many Western actors with different views, and several rounds of reassurance rather than one (p. 341). He lists issues left open: multiple actors, mass publics, asymmetries between the actors and bounded rationality (p. 353). The page draws only the separating equilibrium; the four pooling equilibria are worked out in a formal supplement (p. 337 n. 38).',
  },

  slantchev03: {
    question: 'Why do wars end, usually in a negotiated deal long before either side is destroyed, and how does fighting change what each side will accept (pp. 621-622)?',
    puzzle: 'Wars are costly, so both sides want to end them quickly while conceding as little as possible (p. 621). Yet total disarmament of the loser is rare, and most wars end in settlements short of military collapse (p. 621). If sides fought only until someone won, most wars would end in a victory. The leading answer says war happens because each side is optimistic about winning, so it should end once both agree on who will win (pp. 621-622, 628). Slantchev asks whether that much agreement is really needed.',
    others: [
      { name: 'Mutual optimism', claim: 'Blainey: wars start because at least one side that expected to win turns out wrong, so mutual optimism about victory is needed to start a war. Fearon (1995) and Powell (1996) show how rational states can be optimistic.', take: 'Slantchev calls the emphasis on expectations about the military outcome “misleading” (p. 621). A state can rationally start a war it expects to lose if its opponent thinks it is stronger than it is, because that gets it better terms. So “it is not necessary for both sides to be optimistic about the military outcome” (pp. 628-629).', src: 'pp. 621, 628-629' },
      { name: 'War ends in victory', claim: 'The purpose of military force is victory on the battlefield. MacArthur: “In war there is no substitute for victory.”', take: 'This view “is flawed” because belief in defeat or victory is not a necessary condition for ending a war (p. 628). Political settlements are the substitutes for victory, and complete defeat of one side will be rare (p. 628).', src: 'p. 628' },
      { name: 'War ends when both agree who will win', claim: 'J. Smith and others: fighting continues while each believes in victory, so a clear military trend is needed for a cease-fire.', take: 'The Principle of Convergence is “much weaker”: it “only requires that opponents agree on the relative likelihoods of different outcomes, not on who the eventual winner is going to be” (p. 628).', src: 'p. 628' },
      { name: 'The battlefield is the main source of information', claim: 'Wagner (2000): fighting reveals information not available from bargaining, and is in some ways the better source.', take: 'Wagner “may have overstated his case” because of the fog of war and because bargaining behavior also reveals information (p. 621). In the model, the most important information comes from how opponents bargain: “Combat itself may reveal less than the willingness of opponents to engage in it” (p. 627).', src: 'pp. 621, 627' },
      { name: 'Force works by raising the other side’s costs', claim: 'Wagner (1994): the primary function of force in bargaining is to raise the costs of disagreement for one’s adversary.', take: 'That “does not explain why fighting occurs”: with complete information, states still settle immediately (p. 626).', src: 'p. 626' },
      { name: 'War as a breakdown of bargaining', claim: 'In Fearon (1995), private information creates a risk that bargaining breaks down into war.', take: 'Here war is not a breakdown: it occurs when patient players choose to screen and signal, that is, to transmit information. “War is bargaining, and bargaining is transmission of information” (p. 626).', src: 'p. 626' },
      { name: 'War as a one-shot costly lottery', claim: 'Many bargaining models treat war as a single gamble that ends the game.', take: 'Unlike “the costly lottery approach,” Slantchev’s model does not reduce war to a single-shot event, so it can show how war unfolds (p. 623).', src: 'p. 623' },
      { name: 'Weak states that attack must be irrational', claim: 'When a weak state challenges a much stronger one, we must assume irrational expectations or resolve.', take: 'Not necessary: “as long as the stronger state believes that its opponent is a little stronger than it actually is, the weaker state can benefit from fighting a short war and settling” (p. 622).', src: 'pp. 622, 629' },
    ],
    terms: [
      { term: 'Players 1 and 2', plain: 'Two states at war that bargain over how to split a flow of benefits, for example control of territory. The page’s flow is worth 1 per period, so a share is a number between 0 and 1 (p. 622).' },
      { term: 'Alternating offers', plain: 'Player 1 proposes in one period, player 2 in the next, and so on. This protocol comes from Rubinstein (1982), the last model in this gallery (p. 622).' },
      { term: 'Battle', plain: 'After every rejected offer the two sides fight one engagement. Player 1 wins it with probability p (p. 623).' },
      { term: 'Military position (k)', plain: 'Player 1’s battle wins minus its losses so far. It moves one step up after a player-1 win and one step down after a loss (p. 623). The x-axis of the chart.' },
      { term: 'N (military objectives)', plain: 'The number of steps between the two ends. At k = N player 2 is defeated and player 1 takes everything; at k = 0 the reverse (p. 623).' },
      { term: 'Starting position (k<sub>0</sub>)', plain: 'Where the war begins. Slantchev’s example: Germany’s position against Czechoslovakia was better after Munich handed over the Sudetenland’s fortifications than in 1937 (p. 623 n. 5).' },
      { term: 'p (battle odds)', plain: 'The probability that player 1 wins any one battle. In the complete-information tab both sides know it.' },
      { term: 'δ (discount factor)', plain: 'A number between 0 and 1 for patience: how much a player values a payoff one period later compared with now. Both players share it (p. 622). Near 1 means very patient.' },
      { term: 'b<sub>1</sub>, b<sub>2</sub> (payoff while fighting)', plain: 'What each side gets per period while the war goes on. Fighting is costly, so these are smaller than what the sides get in peace (p. 623).' },
      { term: 'Fight to the finish', plain: 'What a player expects if nobody ever makes a deal and the war runs until one side is defeated (p. 623). The shaded band on the chart holds the deals both sides prefer to that.' },
      { term: 'Offer x<sub>k</sub> and 1 − y<sub>k</sub>', plain: 'x<sub>k</sub> is the share player 1 asks for at position k. y<sub>k</sub> is the share player 2 asks for when it proposes, so 1 − y<sub>k</sub> is what that leaves player 1. Each offer leaves the other side just indifferent between accepting and fighting one more battle (p. 624).' },
      { term: 'Complete information', plain: 'Both sides know everything relevant, including p. Then player 1’s first offer is accepted and no fighting occurs (Proposition 1, p. 624).' },
      { term: 'Subgame perfect and Markov perfect equilibrium', plain: 'Subgame perfect: every threat or promise a player makes is one it would really carry out when the time came. Markov: behavior depends only on the current military position. Slantchev uses both (p. 624).' },
      { term: 'Asymmetric (incomplete) information', plain: 'Only player 2 knows how strong it is. Player 1 has to guess (p. 624). The “Learning while fighting” tab.' },
      { term: 'Type: weak, moderate, strong', plain: 'The three possible kinds of player 2. Against a weak type player 1 wins battles with high odds p<sub>H</sub>; against a moderate one, p<sub>M</sub>; against a strong one, low odds p<sub>L</sub> (p. 624). Note the letters follow player 1’s odds, so the strong type goes with p<sub>L</sub>.' },
      { term: 'q<sub>w</sub>, q<sub>s</sub> (prior)', plain: 'Player 1’s starting beliefs that player 2 is weak or strong. The moderate type gets the rest (p. 624).' },
      { term: 'Belief (posterior) and Bayes’ rule', plain: 'Player 1’s updated probability after seeing what happens. Bayes’ rule is the arithmetic for revising a probability after seeing evidence; here the evidence is who won the battle (p. 624). The page reports player 1’s belief that it faces the strong type.' },
      { term: 'Battlefield vs negotiating table', plain: 'The two sources of information. Battles are “nonmanipulable” but noisy. Offers and refusals are precise but “manipulable,” since a weak state may pretend to be strong (pp. 624, 628).' },
      { term: 'Screening', plain: 'The uninformed side makes an offer that only some types accept, so their answers sort them out (pp. 626-627).' },
      { term: 'Signaling and the non-serious offer', plain: 'The informed side takes a costly action to show its type. Here the strong type makes an offer player 1 is sure to reject, to prove it is willing to keep fighting (p. 626).' },
      { term: 'Separating equilibrium (MPSE)', plain: 'An equilibrium in which each type of player 2 ends up acting differently, so player 1 learns the type. Slantchev’s Proposition 2 describes one for sufficiently patient players: the weak type settles at once, the moderate after one battle, the strong after two (p. 626).' },
      { term: 'Conditions for this equilibrium', plain: 'The checks the page runs, taken from the paper’s appendix. They ask whether each type prefers its part of the equilibrium and whether player 1 prefers screening to settling with all types at once. If any fails, Proposition 2 does not apply at these settings.' },
      { term: 'Semi-separating and pooling equilibria', plain: 'Equilibria where types partly or fully act alike. They appear when players are less patient (p. 626). The page does not compute them.' },
      { term: 'Principle of Convergence', plain: 'Slantchev’s main claim: “warfare ceases to be useful when it loses its informational content,” which happens once both sides can agree on the relative likelihoods of different outcomes (p. 628).' },
    ],
    steps: [
      { t: 'Fighting as a board game', text: 'Start on “Complete information.” Picture a track of N = 6 steps. The war starts in the middle, k<sub>0</sub> = 3; each battle moves it one step, and reaching either end means total defeat for one side. Here both sides know player 1 wins each battle half the time. Player 1 offers a split giving itself 0.52 and player 2 accepts at once. No battle is fought (Proposition 1, p. 624).', set: { v: 'complete', N: 6, k0: 3, p: 0.5, dl: 0.9, b1: 0.3, b2: 0.3 } },
      { t: 'The deal follows the front line', text: 'Start the war near player 1’s victory, at k<sub>0</sub> = 5. Player 1 now asks for 0.80, and it is still accepted at once. The dots on the offer line show player 1’s offer at every position: the better your military position, the better your terms. The shaded band shows all the deals both sides prefer to fighting to the finish.', set: { v: 'complete', N: 6, k0: 5, p: 0.5, dl: 0.9, b1: 0.3, b2: 0.3 } },
      { t: 'Better odds, better terms', text: 'Back at k<sub>0</sub> = 3, raise player 1’s battle odds to p = 0.7. Its offer rises to 0.75. Slantchev: player 1’s proposal is “strictly increasing in p” (p. 624). With complete information, war is a chancy process, yet still no one fights: the chance element alone does not produce war (p. 624).', set: { v: 'complete', N: 6, k0: 3, p: 0.7, dl: 0.9, b1: 0.3, b2: 0.3 } },
      { t: 'Now player 1 must guess', text: 'Switch to “Learning while fighting.” Player 2 may be weak, moderate or strong, and only it knows which. Player 1 opens with an offer only a weak type accepts. Here player 2 is weak and takes it: player 1 gets 0.20. Compare the last line of the panel: had player 1 known it faced a weak state, it would have taken 0.90. “Uncertainty benefits the weak and hurts the strong” (p. 627).', set: { v: 'incomplete', t2: 'w', I0: 1, I1: 1, dl2: 0.99 } },
      { t: 'One battle, then a deal', text: 'Make player 2 moderate. It rejects the opening offer, and one battle is fought. Rejecting signals strength, but the battle is evidence too. If player 1 wins it, its belief that it faces the strong type falls to 31%; switch the first battle to a loss and the belief rises to 51% (eq. 4, p. 631). Either way the moderate type then makes an offer player 1 accepts. Player 1 gets more after winning the battle than after losing it (p. 626).', set: { v: 'incomplete', t2: 'm', I0: 1, I1: 1, dl2: 0.99 } },
      { t: 'The strong type proves itself', text: 'Make player 2 strong. After the first battle it makes a non-serious offer that player 1 is sure to reject, to show it is willing to keep fighting. One more battle follows, and then player 1 makes an offer that is accepted. Two battles, then a deal, and no one was destroyed. The war ended because it had nothing left to reveal (p. 628).', set: { v: 'incomplete', t2: 's', I0: 1, I1: 1, dl2: 0.99 } },
      { t: 'Less patience, a different war', text: 'Lower δ to 0.95. The panel shows one condition failing: the strong type would no longer signal after player 1 loses the first battle. That is the case Slantchev describes, where the strong type pools with the moderate one after a player-1 defeat but still signals after a player-1 win (p. 626). This separating equilibrium exists only for sufficiently patient players (p. 626). Slantchev’s twist on the “shadow of the future”: “The more patient players are, the more incentives they have to delay agreement and fight” (p. 629).', set: { v: 'incomplete', t2: 's', I0: 1, I1: 1, dl2: 0.95 } },
    ],
    answer: 'Wars end when fighting stops revealing information, not when one side becomes sure it will lose (p. 628). Fighting and bargaining both teach each side about the other: battle outcomes cannot be faked but are noisy, while offers and refusals are precise but can be manipulated (p. 628). Once the two sides agree on the relative odds of different outcomes, they settle, on terms that track the military position at the time (pp. 622, 628). So a state need not believe in victory to start a war or in defeat to end one, and a weak state can gain from fighting a little and then settling (pp. 628-629).',
    limits: 'Slantchev notes that some of the negotiating table’s power to reveal information comes from requiring players always to make counteroffers (p. 628). The separating equilibrium exists only for sufficiently patient players; with less patience, semi-separating and pooling equilibria appear (p. 626), which the page does not compute. War does not permanently destroy resources in the model; he conjectures that permanent destruction would make players more reluctant to prolong the learning process (p. 623 n. 7).',
  },

  rubinstein82: {
    question: 'When two parties must agree on how to split something, take turns making offers, and lose value while they wait, what split will rational bargainers reach (pp. 97-98)?',
    puzzle: 'Any split both sides prefer to walking away could in principle be the deal. Before Rubinstein, economists could say little more than that the deal should be no worse than disagreement for either side and that no other deal should be better for both; which of these many deals is chosen was left to vague “bargaining ability” (p. 97). Rubinstein poses the question as: “What ‘will be’ the agreed contract, assuming that both parties behave rationally?” (p. 97). The answer matters well beyond markets: models of war and peace are built on his protocol, including Slantchev’s in this gallery (Slantchev 2003, 622).',
    others: [
      { name: 'The deal is indeterminate', claim: 'Economists could say only that the agreement is individually rational and Pareto optimal, and that which such agreement is chosen “depends on the ‘bargaining ability’ of the parties.”', take: 'Rubinstein sets out to get a clear answer by modeling the bargaining process itself (pp. 97-98).', src: 'p. 97' },
      { name: 'Nash’s axiomatic approach', claim: 'State properties a solution should have and derive the unique solution that satisfies them, without describing how the parties bargain.', take: 'Rubinstein argues that the key axiom in most axiomatizations, the Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives, “has not received a proper defense” and is more suited to asking what a just agreement is than what agreement will be reached (p. 98). He adopts the strategic approach instead (p. 98).', src: 'p. 98' },
      { name: 'Earlier strategic models', claim: 'Nash’s own noncooperative model; Stahl and Krelle’s models with a known, finite number of bargaining periods.', take: 'Nash’s models are “highly stylized and artificial” (p. 98). Stahl’s is the closest to Rubinstein’s, but it has a known finite time horizon and a pie that can be divided only in discrete pieces (p. 99).', src: 'pp. 98-99' },
      { name: 'Nash equilibrium is enough', claim: 'Look for strategies that are each a best reply to the other.', take: 'In this game every possible split can be supported as a Nash equilibrium, so the concept is “weak” (p. 101). The trouble is threats that would not be carried out, which is why Rubinstein requires perfect equilibrium (p. 102).', src: 'pp. 101-102' },
    ],
    terms: [
      { term: 'Pie', plain: 'Whatever is being divided, scaled to size 1. A split, or partition, gives player 1 a share x and player 2 the rest, 1 − x (pp. 98, 100).' },
      { term: 'Players 1 and 2', plain: 'The two bargainers. Player 1 makes the first offer.' },
      { term: 'Alternating offers', plain: 'Player 1 proposes a split. Player 2 accepts, which ends the game, or rejects and makes its own offer next period. Then player 1 accepts or rejects and counter-offers, and so on without limit. No one is bound by its earlier offers (p. 100).' },
      { term: 'Period', plain: 'One round of offer and response. Each rejection costs a period.' },
      { term: 'Discount factor (δ<sub>1</sub>, δ<sub>2</sub>)', plain: 'A number between 0 and 1 for each player’s patience: a share one period later is worth δ times that share now (p. 99). δ near 1 means very patient; δ = 0 means the pie is worthless to that player after the first period (p. 108). The sliders on the “Discounting” tab.' },
      { term: 'Fixed bargaining cost (c<sub>1</sub>, c<sub>2</sub>)', plain: 'The alternative model: each period of delay costs a player a fixed amount c instead of shrinking its share by a fraction (p. 99). The sliders on the “Fixed bargaining costs” tab.' },
      { term: 'Strategy', plain: 'A complete plan: what to offer and what to accept after every possible history of offers, including histories that should never happen (p. 103).' },
      { term: 'Nash equilibrium', plain: 'A pair of strategies where neither player can gain by changing its own strategy alone. In this game every split is a Nash equilibrium, so the concept alone predicts nothing (p. 101).' },
      { term: 'Perfect (subgame perfect) equilibrium', plain: 'A Nash equilibrium in which the plans are also best replies after every possible history, so every threat is one the player would really carry out (p. 99). Rubinstein’s concept, following Selten.' },
      { term: 'Perfect equilibrium partition (P.E.P.)', plain: 'A split that some perfect equilibrium produces (p. 99).' },
      { term: 'M (player 1’s share)', plain: 'With discounting, the unique perfect equilibrium split gives player 1 M = (1 − δ<sub>2</sub>)/(1 − δ<sub>1</sub>δ<sub>2</sub>) (Conclusion 2, p. 108). Player 1’s part of the bar.' },
      { term: 'Why that formula', plain: 'Player 1 offers player 2 exactly what player 2 could get by refusing and proposing next period, discounted by player 2’s patience. Player 2 accepts, since refusing gains nothing. The page’s “Why” box spells out the algebra.' },
      { term: '2’s offer if 2 moved first', plain: 'What player 1 would get if the roles were reversed: δ<sub>1</sub>M. The difference from M shows the edge from moving first.' },
      { term: 'First-mover advantage', plain: 'The gain from making the first offer. With equal patience player 1 gets 1/(1 + δ), more than half, and the edge shrinks as δ approaches 1 (p. 108). The dashed curve on the chart.' },
      { term: 'Immediate agreement', plain: 'In almost all cases the first offer is accepted and no time is lost (p. 107). The possible exception is equal fixed costs.' },
      { term: 'Indeterminate (many equilibria)', plain: 'When the fixed costs are equal, any split giving player 1 at least c<sub>1</sub> is a perfect equilibrium partition, so the model does not pick one (Conclusion 1, p. 107).' },
      { term: 'Complete information', plain: 'Each player knows the other’s patience or costs. Rubinstein calls this “a critical assumption” (p. 99).' },
    ],
    steps: [
      { t: 'The first offer is accepted', text: 'Start on “Discounting” with both players fairly patient, δ<sub>1</sub> = δ<sub>2</sub> = 0.9. Player 1 offers itself 0.53 and player 2 accepts at once. Why accept? Refusing means waiting a period to make its own offer, and waiting shrinks the pie’s value to player 2. Player 1 offers just enough that refusing gains nothing. The bar shows the split (Conclusion 2, p. 108).', set: { v: 'disc', d1: 0.9, d2: 0.9 } },
      { t: 'Impatience helps whoever moves first', text: 'Make both players impatient, δ = 0.5. Player 1’s share rises to 0.67, which is 1/(1 + δ). Delay is so costly that player 2 accepts a much smaller share rather than wait. Rubinstein notes the solution “gives relative advantage to the player who starts the bargaining” (p. 99).', set: { v: 'disc', d1: 0.5, d2: 0.5 } },
      { t: 'Patience erases the first-mover edge', text: 'Now make both very patient, δ = 0.99. Player 1’s share falls to about 0.503, which the page rounds to 0.50: barely more than half. “As one would expect, 1’s gain from the fact that he starts the bargaining decreases as δ tends to 1” (p. 108). On the chart the dashed curve 1/(1 + δ) approaches one half.', set: { v: 'disc', d1: 0.99, d2: 0.99 } },
      { t: 'Patience is power', text: 'Set player 1 impatient (δ<sub>1</sub> = 0.5) and player 2 very patient (δ<sub>2</sub> = 0.99). Player 1, although it moves first, gets only 0.02. Player 2 can credibly wait, so player 1 must give it almost everything. The solution is “monotonic in the discounting factors” (p. 99): drag δ<sub>1</sub> across the chart and player 1’s share rises with its own patience.', set: { v: 'disc', d1: 0.5, d2: 0.99 } },
      { t: 'A responder with no future', text: 'Set δ<sub>2</sub> = 0: to player 2, pie next period is worth nothing. Then player 2 “has no threat,” and player 1 takes the whole pie (p. 108).', set: { v: 'disc', d1: 0.5, d2: 0 } },
      { t: 'Fixed costs: the cheaper waiter wins', text: 'Switch to “Fixed bargaining costs.” Each period of delay costs player 1 c<sub>1</sub> = 0.1 and player 2 c<sub>2</sub> = 0.2. Since delay hurts player 2 more, player 1 gets the whole pie (Conclusion 1, p. 107).', set: { v: 'cost', c1: 0.1, c2: 0.2 } },
      { t: 'Fixed costs: the dearer waiter loses', text: 'Reverse the costs: c<sub>1</sub> = 0.2 and c<sub>2</sub> = 0.1. Player 1 gets only 0.1, what player 2 would lose by waiting one round. Rubinstein: the weaker player “can at most get the loss which his opponent incurs during one bargaining round” (p. 99).', set: { v: 'cost', c1: 0.2, c2: 0.1 } },
      { t: 'Equal costs: no single answer', text: 'Make the costs equal, 0.1 each. Now any split giving player 1 at least 0.1 is a perfect equilibrium partition (Conclusion 1, p. 107). With equal costs the model does not pin down the deal, and Rubinstein shows an equilibrium here in which agreement comes only in the second period (pp. 107-108).', set: { v: 'cost', c1: 0.1, c2: 0.1 } },
    ],
    answer: 'Requiring that every threat be credible picks out a single deal in most cases, and it is struck at once (pp. 99, 107). With discounting, player 1 gets (1 − δ<sub>2</sub>)/(1 − δ<sub>1</sub>δ<sub>2</sub>): each side’s share rises with its own patience, and moving first helps less the more patient both are (pp. 99, 108). With fixed costs, the side whose delay costs less gets nearly everything, and the other gets at most its opponent’s one-round cost (pp. 99, 107).',
    limits: 'The results assume each player knows the other’s preferences, which Rubinstein calls “a critical assumption.” If one side’s costs were private, he expects that bargaining would last more than one period, and he left that case for another paper (pp. 99-100). With equal fixed costs the model does not pin down the split, and an equilibrium can even delay agreement and fail to be Pareto optimal (pp. 107-108).',
  },
};
